WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.300
- I say in the book, when
I walked in that room,

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.630
I knew 15 of the other 16 Republicans

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.463
who were running for president.

00:00:06.463 --> 00:00:08.600
I mean, I knew Marco
Rubio, I know John Kasich,

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.950
I knew Jeb Bush, I had met them all.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.160
The only one I had never
met was Donald Trump,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.410
so I was kinda curious.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.190
And I say in the book,
when I walked in that room,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.750
I did not like him, I
did not like his persona,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.200
I thought he was kind of a blowhard.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.590
I thought his run for presidency

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.630
was a publicity stunt, frankly.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.607
And we spent about an hour
and 20 minutes with him,

00:00:27.607 --> 00:00:30.303
and I walked out with stars in my eyes.

00:00:30.303 --> 00:00:33.550
(intense music)

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.280
- I'm Dave Rubin, and
this the "Rubin Report."

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.440
Quick reminder to subscribe and make sure

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.340
that you get notified of our videos.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.280
Why not, right?

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.780
Okay, joining me today
is an author, economist,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.400
and former advisor to President Trump.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.500
Steve Moore, welcome
to the "Rubin Report."

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.540
- Hi, Dave, thanks for having me.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:48.790
- I am glad to have you here.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.250
We're gonna have to talk somewhat quick

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.510
because you're in a
major time crunch here.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.940
- It's an honor to be on the show.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:54.860
- I'm thrilled to have you here.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.520
This is gonna be a little
quicker than my average show,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.850
so I usually do a little--
- Rapid fire, all right.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.250
- I do a little backstory usually.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.430
I try to find out where people are from

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.280
and all sorts of stuff.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.670
We're gonna do that next
time I sit down with you.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.020
Let's go right into economics.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.880
As a "Wall Street Journal"
guy and a longtime economist,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.570
and you studied at George
Mason, and all of this stuff,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.960
how would you say your...

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.580
what would you say your
economic principles are?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.270
Let's just do 101, Moore economics.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.220
- Well, I believe in
freedom and free markets.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.580
I believe that as Thomas Jefferson said,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.170
that government which least governs best,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.060
and get the government for the most part

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.920
out of the way of the economy,
and it'll do just fine.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.780
That's not to say we
shouldn't have regulation.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.010
We should certainly have smart...

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.750
We want clean air and clean
water and safe workplaces.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.360
We wanna a tax system
that raises the revenue

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.740
the government needs, but in a
way that is non-confiscatory.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.426
Are you listening, Elizabeth Warren?

00:01:46.426 --> 00:01:47.777
- (laughs) We'll talk
about her in a little bit.

00:01:47.777 --> 00:01:49.470
- And it doesn't distort the economy.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.840
So I've been a flat tax
guy for all my life.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.160
I love the idea, let's just
have a really low rate,

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.470
everybody pays it.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.563
You make 10 times more money than I do,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.760
you pay 10 times more tax,
and not 50 times more tax,

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.000
and get rid of all the
loopholes in the tax system.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.350
So I believe the free enterprise system

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.400
is the goose that lays the golden eggs.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.560
- All right, so I'm basically
with you on all of that,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.130
but let's do the flat tax for a second.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:15.380
So I would be basically for something

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.210
like 15% for everybody.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.470
Would you do something to help the people

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.900
that are at the lowest--
- Yes.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.170
- So a certain amount of
people would pay nothing.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.360
- Yeah, so like in the
standard flat tax plan,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.580
you said 15%, I'd love that,
but most plans are about 18%,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.810
and then you basically
say for a family of four,

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:34.000
roughly your first say
$30,000 earned is tax free,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.820
and then above that amount, you pay a tax.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:38.750
So people below the poverty line

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.600
are not paying any federal income tax.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.450
I think most people regard
that as fair and equitable

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.890
and very pro-growth and
would love to see it.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.833
The reason it doesn't happen

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:50.430
is not even because liberals oppose it,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.280
it's the special interest
groups in Washington.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.030
Do you think they wanna a flat tax?

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:56.730
- The lobbyists make money

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.940
buying and selling favors in the tax code.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.410
If you have a flat tax, what
do they have to sell and buy?

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.243
- No more favors.

00:03:02.243 --> 00:03:05.040
- If you really wanna drain
the swamp, go to a flat tax.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.155
- Would you do anything on
the really high side of that?

00:03:08.155 --> 00:03:09.173
(laughing)

00:03:09.173 --> 00:03:10.277
- So right now, you
mentioned Elizabeth Warren.

00:03:10.277 --> 00:03:12.120
- We'd be good at 19%.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.820
- Is there something you would do,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.300
even if it didn't work sort
of economically in your mind,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:20.767
just to kinda throw the
progressives a bone?

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:21.600
- You know why I don't like it?

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.750
I get it, I get asked that question a lot.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:23.920
You know why I don't like that?

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.417
'Cause you say, "Well,
why don't we just have 20%

00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:29.166
"for the really rich?"

00:03:29.166 --> 00:03:31.277
And then Elizabeth Warren
says, "What about 25?

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:32.650
"Why not 30, why not..."

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.240
We had tax rates at one
point in this country at 91%.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.350
So I don't like that idea,
'cause I think it perverts the...

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.620
I love the idea everybody
pays the same rate.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.440
No, the idea, oh, we're
just gonna have a surcharge

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.880
on the Bill Gates and the
Warren Buffets of the world,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.420
they will pay their tax.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.940
When they earn their
money, they will pay it.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.230
And by the way, those
are people who employ

00:03:54.151 --> 00:03:55.820
in some cases hundreds
of thousands of workers.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.820
They pay tax, so I wanna retain
the virtue and simplicity

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:02.910
of a one-rate system.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:06.100
- Right, those guys are also
always for raising taxes,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.078
and yet they don't voluntarily pay more.

00:04:08.078 --> 00:04:08.911
- Yeah, isn't that interesting?

00:04:08.911 --> 00:04:11.031
- What do you make of the
mind of someone like that?

00:04:11.031 --> 00:04:12.480
- Well, there's a group called
the Patriotic Millionaires.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.560
There's something like 100
millionaires and billionaires

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.110
who have signed something
saying, "We wanna pay more tax."

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.090
I call them the hypocritical millionaires

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.090
because there's nothing
to prevent somebody...

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:24.600
At the bottom line of your tax form is

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.500
if you wanna pay more
money into the Treasury,

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.720
you can write a check,
and none of them do that.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.460
So my feeling is that
they want other people

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.750
to pay more tax, but they don't
wanna pay more themselves.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.410
And then they say, "Well, but
the tax system's involuntary,"

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.640
and I'm like, wait a minute,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.690
you know people voluntarily wanna serve,

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:43.950
people sign up for the military.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.130
Those are patriotic people,
they care about our country,

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.339
and they're willing to give

00:04:48.339 --> 00:04:50.870
potentially their life for our country.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.520
But these quote, "patriotic
millionaires" won't pay

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.700
more tax themselves, but
they want other people

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:56.533
to pay more tax.

00:04:56.533 --> 00:04:57.740
- Does it become one of those odd things

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.120
where it's like the CEO
of Walmart or something

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:03.520
will publicly say that he's
for a $15 minimum wage,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:06.420
where he knows actually it
won't ultimately hurt him,

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:08.089
but it'll hurt--
- That's a great point.

00:05:08.089 --> 00:05:09.950
- All the small businesses that
won't be able to afford it.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.430
He'll be able to afford it.
- Oh, yeah.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.290
That's why a lot of the
regulations in Washington

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.560
that liberals want,
they are able to team up

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.490
with the big companies,

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.000
because the big companies
can absorb the cost

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.010
of requiring a company to
pay healthcare benefits

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:28.020
or education benefits or
raise the minimum wage,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.760
so it's a way actually of
strengthening the big guys

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.360
and strangling the small guys

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.140
who are trying to compete
with the big guys.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.510
The small businesses are the
ones who get hurt the most

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.680
by those kinds of regulations.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.050
- Right, it's interesting
that you mentioned

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:45.010
in relation to tax that even
if you threw somebody a bone,

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:46.240
it's never enough kind of,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.380
which reminds me of the
$15 minimum wage thing,

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.210
where Rashida Tliab said,
"Well, why not $20?"

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.480
And then it's like, well, Bernie or Warren

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.510
or the rest of them, they have
nothing to really stand on

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:58.030
to say why it should be 15 and not 20,

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.989
'cause once you artificially
put these numbers in--

00:05:59.989 --> 00:06:02.254
- By the way, I'm for the highest wages

00:06:02.254 --> 00:06:04.030
Americans can possibly get.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.690
- So how do you ensure that then

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:07.640
without doing too much
government involvement?

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:09.490
- Well, you don't have to
have the government do it.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.870
First of all, one of the principles,

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.160
you asked me about
principles of economics,

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.510
one good principle is you
never want the government

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.270
setting prices or setting wages.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.010
That's supposed to be done by
the free enterprise system,

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.520
the law of supply and demand.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.910
And when you have the
government come in and set...

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.640
I'll give you an example in a sec.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.610
You are living here in Los Angeles.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:29.630
In a lotta cities, you have rent control

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:34.390
to try to make housing more
affordable for poor people.

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.350
Look, we all want people to have shelter

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.770
and a roof over their head.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.550
But it turns out, this
is an perfect example

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.520
of the law of unintended consequences.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:45.930
It turns out when you
actually impose rent control,

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.390
you actually reduce the
amount of affordable housing

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.440
and you actually have more
people homeless, not less.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:51.800
And New York City found that,

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.557
and yet liberals seem to
never understand that,

00:06:55.557 --> 00:06:57.240
and it's the same thing with,

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.900
what were you asking me about, the--

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.732
- Well, the minimum wage.
- Yeah, the minimum wage.

00:07:00.732 --> 00:07:02.600
- It's like it's not
hinged to reality sort of,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.480
so once someone says it should be 15,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.524
well, then why not 20,
and then why not 25?

00:07:06.524 --> 00:07:07.500
- Yeah, exactly, and
so what tends to happen

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.130
when you raise the minimum
wage is you actually,

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:11.980
the people who tend to get hurt the most

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.483
are the people with the least skill,

00:07:13.483 --> 00:07:15.520
because you essentially
price them outta the market.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.390
I always tell the story,
I have two teenage sons,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.010
I love them to death, but I'd
never pay them $15 an hour,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:22.377
right?
- Right.

00:07:22.377 --> 00:07:23.350
- I mean, they don't have
the training and skills

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:26.580
to warrant a $15 a hour wage.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.820
Why not, especially for young people,

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:32.520
let them work for $7 or $8
an hour and gain the skills.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.760
I remember very well, by
the way, my first job.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.500
I think most people listening to this,

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.110
watching the show, remember.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:39.804
Do you remember the first job you ever had

00:07:39.804 --> 00:07:40.637
where you got a paycheck?
- I do.

00:07:40.637 --> 00:07:42.707
- What was that?
- I was a paperboy.

00:07:42.707 --> 00:07:44.079
- A paperboy, okay.
- I was a paperboy.

00:07:44.079 --> 00:07:45.160
- I was working in a small warehouse

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:46.633
in Northbrook, Illinois.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.590
But you learn a lot with your first job,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.400
how to show up on time, how
to be nice to be your boss,

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.850
how to learn some skills,

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.980
so you learn that whether
you're delivering newspapers

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:58.830
or whether you're working in a factory.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.200
So I don't like that about it.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.960
I like letting the wages rise,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.510
and that's happened
under Trump, by the way.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:07.960
We've gotten really nice
wage gains under Trump

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:09.160
through the free enterprise system

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:11.450
in creating a very tight labor market.

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:14.060
Workers have a lot of
bargaining power right now.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.080
- So you advised Trump on the tax cuts

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.930
a couple of years ago.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.070
Talk to me just a little
bit about what's that like,

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.360
when you're sitting in
a room with this guy?

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.610
Are his guiding principles
sort of economically

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:26.740
what you've kinda laid out?

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.383
He's obviously a capitalist.

00:08:28.383 --> 00:08:29.760
- Do you know Trump?

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.593
- I've never met him personally.

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:33.790
- So he's a really good guy.

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:36.350
Believe it or not, I
always say half-jokingly

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.810
that most politicians are
wonderful people in public

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:39.890
and jerks in private.

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:41.320
Trump can be a jerk in public,

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.210
but he is a wonderful person in private.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:44.610
I like him a lot.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.020
He's interesting, he's charming,

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:48.940
he's attentive to what you're saying.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:50.310
He looks at you straight in the eye

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.773
and he wants to know,
and he'll combat you.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.774
I like that, he'll joust with you

00:08:55.774 --> 00:08:56.890
if he doesn't agree with your position,

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.350
which he'll have four or
five people in the room

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.590
and they'll argue, and that's
what good CEO should do,

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.670
not just surround themselves with yes men,

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.230
but people who will argue out the point.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.867
- So when you guys sat
down and he's going,

00:09:06.867 --> 00:09:09.407
"I wanna cut taxes and this is
one of the things I promised.

00:09:09.407 --> 00:09:11.150
"Now help me pull this thing together."

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.831
What's that like?
- I'll give you one example.

00:09:12.831 --> 00:09:15.350
This tells you a lot about the tax bill,

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:16.700
but it also tells you about Donald Trump.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.470
So I remember we went into
Trump, this was Larry Kudlow,

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:20.890
and now Larry's now the chief economist,

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.260
and we said, "Hey," we
showed him this chart

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:27.860
that had the U.S. tax rate
on our businesses was 40%

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:31.210
and the rest of the world
was on average about 20%.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:33.620
And we said, "You know,
this is a problem for us."

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.960
As Larry called it, it's a 20% headstart

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.320
for all the countries we're competing with

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.061
'cause they've got a lower
penalty that they face.

00:09:41.061 --> 00:09:42.990
As a result of that,
companies were leaving

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:44.780
the United States and
they were going to Ireland

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:46.790
or they were going to Canada
or they were going to Mexico.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.270
This was called inversions,

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.460
it's where companies were giving up

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.380
their American citizenship
to move somewhere else.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:52.827
So anyway, we said to Trump,

00:09:52.827 --> 00:09:56.560
"We think you should have a
20% rate in your tax plan."

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.647
And I'll never forget,
he sat back and he said,

00:09:58.647 --> 00:10:00.420
"No, I'm not gonna do that."

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.340
We said, "Well, why?"

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:02.720
He said, "I want 15%."

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.790
And what was so interesting about that

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.860
was that from that moment
until we passed the bill

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:12.225
in late December of 2017,
he always insisted on 15%,

00:10:12.225 --> 00:10:13.550
whether it's a meeting
with Mitch McConnell

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:17.420
or Paul Ryan or any public
declaration, 15, 15, 15.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:18.870
And I'll never forget, we were waiting

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.530
for the final vote in the Senate,

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.710
'cause we needed to get 50 votes
in the Senate to pass this,

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.330
we were nervous about
whether we could this.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:28.250
Remember, on Obamacare,
we came up with 49 votes,

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:31.100
one vote short, so we didn't
wanna come up with 49 again.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.800
So we're very nervous,
we're waiting for McConnell

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.480
to come back from his come-to-Jesus moment

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.640
when they had their final meeting

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.760
in the Senate Republican caucus
to see if they had 50 votes.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:41.910
And I remember he walks in,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.507
he goes right up to the
president, and he said,

00:10:43.507 --> 00:10:45.667
"Mr. President, I'm so
sorry, I failed you.

00:10:45.667 --> 00:10:48.177
"I know you wanted that 15% tax rate.

00:10:48.177 --> 00:10:50.503
"I made every argument I possibly could,

00:10:51.387 --> 00:10:53.397
"and I failed you, Mr. President.

00:10:53.397 --> 00:10:54.230
"What can I say?

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:56.627
"I just couldn't convince my colleagues.

00:10:56.627 --> 00:10:58.620
"I said I couldn't get you that 15%."

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.410
He said, "Would you take 20%."

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.400
Well, Trump went, "Yeah,
I'll take that in a minute."

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.240
I really believe the
really interesting thing

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.977
about that story is I always thank God,

00:11:07.977 --> 00:11:09.157
"Thank you, God, that Donald Trump

00:11:09.157 --> 00:11:10.520
"didn't listen to Larry and I."

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:12.690
He does understand the art of the deal.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.840
I think he wanted 20% all along,

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.930
but he understood if
you wanna end up at 20%,

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.570
that's not your opening bid, right?

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.550
So he started at 15, and I guarantee you,

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.940
I guarantee you if he had started at 20%

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.930
like Larry and I had,
we would have ended up

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:28.770
at about 27 or 28%.

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:30.120
It's because he started at 15,

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.170
the negotiated position was 20.

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:34.996
Well, we ended up actually at
21%, but that's pretty good.

00:11:34.996 --> 00:11:35.829
- Do you think basically

00:11:35.829 --> 00:11:37.990
that is his secret sauce to everything?

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:38.960
I mean, we could even take this

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:40.300
outside of the world of economics,

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.440
just that negotiating
like that is basically,

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:46.190
that is his greatest skill, period.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.047
- Well, you know the
name of his book, right?

00:11:48.047 --> 00:11:49.450
- "The Art of the Deal."
- "The Art of the Deal."

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:51.840
He's a good negotiator, he is.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:53.830
I've seen him many times in person

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:55.403
and he knows how to negotiate.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.440
That's what's made him
successful in business.

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:00.690
Look, he's made mistakes as president.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.410
I'm not an apologist for Trump.

00:12:02.410 --> 00:12:05.480
I like where we are with the economy,

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.340
but yeah, I think this
is one of his assets,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.520
he's a very good negotiator.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.050
- So where are we exactly
right now with the economy?

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:12.532
- Good, it's good.

00:12:12.532 --> 00:12:13.989
- Depending on who you listen to,

00:12:13.989 --> 00:12:14.822
it seems like we're all over the place.

00:12:14.822 --> 00:12:15.655
- Well, first of all, let me say this.

00:12:15.655 --> 00:12:17.410
If I had told you three years ago

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:19.360
that after say three years of Trump,

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:20.750
not quite three, but we're good,

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.730
we would have the lowest
unemployment rate in 50 years,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.400
the lowest inflation rate in 50 years,

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.110
the lowest interest rates in 50 years,

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.240
and the biggest wage gains in 30 years,

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:31.817
that's a pretty darn good economy,

00:12:31.817 --> 00:12:33.350
and that's what we have right now.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:37.460
We have 7.1 million surplus
jobs in the United States.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:38.370
Think about that.

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.781
That's never happened before
in the history of our country.

00:12:40.781 --> 00:12:43.600
7.1 million unfilled jobs,

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.860
that's larger than the entire population

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.910
of the state of Indiana.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:48.531
That's how many surplus jobs.

00:12:48.531 --> 00:12:51.990
What that means is workers
have bargaining power.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.440
It goes back to what
we were saying before.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.150
So you don't like your
job working for Joe's,

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:58.720
you can go down the
street and work for Susan

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:00.610
and she's gonna give you a higher offer.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:04.390
We just had negotiations
between the UAW and GM.

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:07.850
The UAW, it looks like
they've gotten pretty much

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:10.530
what they wanted in
these negotiations, why?

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.460
Because it's not like UAW
can go find other workers,

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:15.350
there's a shortage of them.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:18.210
So I think it's been a
positive thing for workers.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.510
Let me summarize, if you
are an American worker

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:22.900
and you are looking for a job,

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.120
and you have some kind of useful skill,

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:28.340
this is the best labor market
in any of our lifetimes.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.970
It's really good.

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:30.803
Can it continue?

00:13:30.803 --> 00:13:32.070
I think it can.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.570
- So you mentioned regulation before,

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:36.580
how do you figure out
where the moments are

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.570
that you have to use
the touch of government,

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:41.470
because as you said, you are
for some level of regulation?

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:44.117
- The way I like to put it is,

00:13:44.117 --> 00:13:47.635
look, we all believe in, not
all of us, but most of us--

00:13:47.635 --> 00:13:48.468
(laughing)

00:13:48.468 --> 00:13:49.570
Be careful.
- In sound regulation

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:53.020
that makes the economy
run and stops cheaters

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:53.853
and things like that.

00:13:53.853 --> 00:13:55.593
- I have a lot of Libertarians
that watch this show though.

00:13:55.593 --> 00:13:56.426
- There's some who want no regulation.

00:13:56.426 --> 00:13:57.370
- Just by saying the word regulation,

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:58.203
a lot of them--
- I have friends

00:13:58.203 --> 00:13:59.700
who don't even believe
you should have to drive

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.271
on the right side of the road.

00:14:01.271 --> 00:14:02.104
(laughing)

00:14:02.104 --> 00:14:02.937
Good luck.

00:14:02.937 --> 00:14:06.010
My point is that I believe regulations

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:09.600
should be guardrails on the road,

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.013
so to keep you on the road.

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:15.310
The left believes that
a lot of the regulations

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.820
should be roadblocks to
prevent people from...

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.560
We want our businesses to succeed,

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.600
but we wanna them to stay
on the straight and narrow.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.030
Too many times, I saw this
in the Obama administration,

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:26.020
too many times the regulations

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:29.537
were just meant to bring these
companies to an abrupt stop,

00:14:29.537 --> 00:14:30.730
and I think that's the difference.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.480
We want regulation that makes
sense, that's cost effective,

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:38.020
and that achieves its mission
without disrupting our economy

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:39.330
in a way that will destroy jobs.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.210
- Can you give me an
example of a regulation

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:42.390
that Trump has cut?

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.580
There was the original, wasn't
there that idea at one point

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:46.700
that for every new
regulation he was gonna pass,

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:49.200
he was gonna cut something,
like 70 regulations?

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.820
- Two, he said, "I want every
new one, we get rid of two,"

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:53.290
and actually so far for every new one,

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:54.123
we've kinda removed 20.

00:14:54.123 --> 00:14:55.720
So he's been a big deregulator.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:57.810
- So can you give me an example
of where something like that

00:14:57.810 --> 00:14:59.547
that he's cut has actually
helped the economy?

00:14:59.547 --> 00:15:02.263
- Well, the best example
is in the energy area.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:08.330
Barack Obama and his team
hated the fossil fuel industry.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:09.870
They hated shale, oil, and gas.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.470
They hated the coal producers.

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.950
So they passed regulations that were,

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:18.930
by the way, the law says that
regulations should be passed

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:22.680
in a way where they're
achievable for an industry.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.840
In other words, they shouldn't be designed

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:26.557
to bankrupt a company.
- To take out an industry.

00:15:26.557 --> 00:15:30.490
- But in the area of coal,
the intention was precisely

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.740
to just put the coal
producers outta business,

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:34.330
and it hurt our own gas.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.470
Now we've got a situation
where we're producing

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.830
more oil and gas and
coal than ever before,

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.320
we're producing more nuclear power,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:43.300
and that's created for
the first time in our,

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.540
again, in your or my lifetime,
we're actually producing

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.640
more oil and gas than we're consuming.

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:50.470
I mean, that's a pretty
cool thing to think about.

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:55.243
We're no longer susceptible
to Arab oil embargoes

00:15:55.243 --> 00:15:58.554
or the Russian influence.

00:15:58.554 --> 00:16:01.900
You saw what happened when the Iranians

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.530
bombed the oil fields in Saudi Arabia.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.860
That was, what, about
three or four weeks ago.

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:09.010
If that had happened
before the shale revolution

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:11.160
that we have going on,
the price of gasoline...

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:13.740
Well, what are you paying
here in California?

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:14.573
You pay a lot for gas.

00:16:14.573 --> 00:16:15.680
- I don't even know what it is.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:16.750
I have an electric car now,

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:17.637
so I'm not so sure.
- It's like 4 or $5 a gallon.

00:16:17.637 --> 00:16:19.260
But on the East Coast, where I live,

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:21.110
you pay maybe $3 a gallon.

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:22.410
The price of gas would probably have gone

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.400
to about $6 a gallon if it
hadn't been for the fact

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:27.630
that the United States
is actually now producing

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:29.130
a lot of oil and gas ourselves.

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:32.470
- So do you think that the
primary role of the government

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:34.970
basically is to make sure that
the economy is running right?

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.320
Is that really the first thing

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.080
that the government has to do?
- It's to protect our life

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:41.470
and our liberty, and
the reason we have laws

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:46.470
is to make sure that I
don't violate your rights

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:48.623
and you don't violate my rights.

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:54.890
I'm kind of old-fashioned, I
believe if I'm doing something

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:59.890
that doesn't interfere with
your rights to life and liberty,

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:01.550
that you should be able to do it.

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:05.560
I don't like laws that
are intended, for example,

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:07.713
to protect me from myself.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.830
I think adults should be
able to do pretty much...

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:14.153
I don't like drugs, I don't like tobacco,

00:17:15.013 --> 00:17:16.650
I don't smoke, I don't do drugs.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:18.840
But if you wanna do drugs, I
think you're stupid to do them.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.672
- No comment, no comment.

00:17:20.672 --> 00:17:21.692
(laughing)

00:17:21.692 --> 00:17:22.560
But I don't think the
government should be involved

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:23.983
in those kind of decisions.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:27.957
- What else should we know about Trump

00:17:27.957 --> 00:17:29.410
as someone that has
spent some time with him?

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:31.450
Not even the negotiating part,

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:32.480
I mean, to give us a little insight

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:34.320
into just the human part.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:35.830
But it's a little hard
to just figure it out

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:36.663
through the media.

00:17:36.663 --> 00:17:38.761
- One of the things that
impressed me the most

00:17:38.761 --> 00:17:39.900
when I wrote the book, "Trumponomics"

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:41.210
with my friend, Art Laffer,

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.870
the first few chapters that
I recommend people read

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.720
are just if they're interested
in how Trump operates

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:48.660
is to read those, because
we really talk about

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:53.430
how Trump just as a
person, what's he like.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:54.870
One of the things that first impressed me

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.950
when we were called by Corey Lewandowski

00:17:57.950 --> 00:17:59.910
was running the campaign back then,

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.280
and will go down in the history books

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:04.053
for getting Trump through
that Republican nomination,

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:07.917
Trump called us in something
like January of 2016

00:18:07.917 --> 00:18:10.267
and said, "Mr. Trump
would like to meet you

00:18:10.267 --> 00:18:12.890
"and Larry Kudlow at Trump Tower."

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:14.640
So I was curious.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.590
The Trump movement was
just sort of getting going

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.307
at that point, but you could
sense something was going on,

00:18:18.307 --> 00:18:20.800
and I wanted to see what it was all about.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:24.340
I say in the book, when
I walked in that room,

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:26.273
I knew 15 of the other 16 Republicans

00:18:26.273 --> 00:18:27.290
that were running for president.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:29.590
I knew Marco Rubio, I know John Kasich,

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.010
I knew Jeb Bush, I had met them all.

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.180
The only one I had never
met was Donald Trump,

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:35.430
so I was kinda curious.

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.210
And I say in the book,
when I walked in that room,

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.770
I did not like him, I
did not like his persona,

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:42.210
I thought he was kind of a blowhard.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:43.600
I thought his run for presidency

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.660
was a publicity stunt, frankly.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:48.617
And we spent about an hour
and 20 minutes with him,

00:18:48.617 --> 00:18:50.370
and I walked out with stars in my eyes.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.433
He's an incredibly charming
and charismatic person,

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:57.840
and he's always personally
been very good to me.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:01.040
He doesn't throw people under
the bus that are loyal to him,

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:06.040
and I've tried to be loyal to
him, and it goes both ways.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.140
When I went through the
fed reserve nomination

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:13.010
and just got pulverized by all
these accusations against me,

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.860
I remember I would call Trump up,

00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:15.693
call the president and say,

00:19:15.693 --> 00:19:18.497
"Mr. President, if this
is a problem for you,

00:19:18.497 --> 00:19:21.037
"that I hate black
people and I hate midgets

00:19:21.037 --> 00:19:24.080
"and I hate Jews, and
all these allegations,"

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:27.467
and I said, "Look, if this is a problem,

00:19:27.467 --> 00:19:28.457
"I'm happy to step down."

00:19:28.457 --> 00:19:30.170
- You do realize that that's the clip

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:31.521
that people are gonna put online.

00:19:31.521 --> 00:19:32.354
(laughing)

00:19:32.354 --> 00:19:33.270
- Yeah, I know, but let
me be very clear, folks,

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.313
I don't hate Jews, I don't hate blacks,

00:19:35.313 --> 00:19:36.146
I don't hate midgets.

00:19:36.146 --> 00:19:38.064
I'm saying that's the kind of thing

00:19:38.064 --> 00:19:38.897
they were saying about me.

00:19:38.897 --> 00:19:41.927
But anyway, I said, "Mr. President,

00:19:41.927 --> 00:19:43.077
"if that's a problem for you,

00:19:43.077 --> 00:19:44.170
"I don't wanna embarrass you."

00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:47.840
He said, he was, "Get back
there, keep fighting."

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.710
And one thing I do respect about Trump,

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:54.100
and look, a lot of the
things he does and says,

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:54.933
I don't like at all.

00:19:54.933 --> 00:19:56.860
I wish he would stop
tweeting, for example.

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:00.500
But what I went through was
for about three or four weeks

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:02.260
of just getting pulverized in the media.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:03.857
Finally, my wife said,
"If you stay in this,

00:20:03.857 --> 00:20:05.206
"I'm gonna divorce you."
- Wow.

00:20:05.206 --> 00:20:06.190
(laughing)

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:09.110
- And so I just said, okay,
I can't take this anymore.

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:12.550
Trump has taken 1,000 times what I took,

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:14.420
and day after day after day after day,

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:16.330
and yet he never lets...

00:20:18.052 --> 00:20:20.845
It just wore me down,
but he doesn't get down.

00:20:20.845 --> 00:20:21.850
- How do you think he does that?

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:25.370
- He's like a superhero (laughs)
or something in the sense.

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.510
It's like he's got a coat
of armor or something

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:32.510
that protects him from
these insults and stuff.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:34.190
He's always called a racist,

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:35.683
and that's the other thing I found.

00:20:35.683 --> 00:20:39.300
I couldn't believe how
many blacks and minorities

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:41.570
he had working for him,
and they loved him.

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:43.980
So he's not a perfect person by any means,

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:46.330
he's got a lot of faults,
but I think he's a...

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:50.190
The one thing you always
ask me, what drives him?

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:53.080
And it really is, how do we
make America great again,

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:55.870
and how do we make sure
that the economy is working

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.600
for middle class blue-collar workers?

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:01.060
'Cause those are the
people who elected Trump.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.970
Those were the people
in Erie, Pennsylvania,

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:07.250
and Lansing, Michigan,
and Oshkosh, Wisconsin,

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:08.830
and Charleston, West Virginia,

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:12.220
who frankly were pissed
off at both parties,

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:13.340
Democrats and Republicans.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.365
They didn't like the Bush years,

00:21:15.365 --> 00:21:16.735
they didn't like the Obama years.

00:21:16.735 --> 00:21:19.040
And Trump knows the people
who brought him to this dance.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:20.270
- Do you think there's any risk

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:24.877
in having a sorta pure businessman
as the chief executive?

00:21:24.877 --> 00:21:26.710
- I think it's a breath of fresh air.

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:29.110
I think that's the reason he won.

00:21:29.110 --> 00:21:30.640
I think people were sick of politicians.

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:33.420
Remember, what was his closing
argument in the election?

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.110
Do you remember the campaign?

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:36.240
- Well, there were a couple of things.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:37.273
One of them is what do you have to lose?

00:21:37.273 --> 00:21:38.106
(laughing)

00:21:38.106 --> 00:21:38.939
- Yeah, that one was good.

00:21:38.939 --> 00:21:39.772
- That was one of the big ones.

00:21:39.772 --> 00:21:41.750
- The other one was, drain the swamp.

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.420
And I gotta tell you, that
really appealed to people

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.510
around the country because Washington,

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.270
this is one of my little pet peeves

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:50.270
and Trump tapped into this,

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:55.270
Washington is getting rich at
the expense of the rest of us.

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:58.320
Three of the five wealthiest
counties in the United States

00:21:58.320 --> 00:21:59.490
are in or around Washington,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:00.410
did you know that?
- Yeah.

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:02.634
- Three of the five, how did we do that?

00:22:02.634 --> 00:22:04.770
I've lived in Washington for 30 years.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.540
We don't produce anything.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:09.420
All we produce is laws,
learners, politicians,

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:11.580
rules, regulations, and we're getting rich

00:22:11.580 --> 00:22:13.360
at the expense of the rest of the country.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:14.193
And you know what?

00:22:14.193 --> 00:22:16.230
The American people finally caught on.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:17.180
It's like, wait a minute,

00:22:17.180 --> 00:22:18.750
we're sending all our money to Washington,

00:22:18.750 --> 00:22:21.770
we send them a trillion dollars,

00:22:21.770 --> 00:22:23.710
they send us $900 billion back,

00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:25.160
and they keep the rest of the money.

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:26.930
And I think that's something

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:28.280
that I really admire about Trump.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:30.080
He is trying to drain the swamp.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:31.653
But boy, is that tough to do.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:34.390
- Okay, so some of the
principles you laid out here

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:36.280
strike me as a lotta common sense stuff,

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:38.500
and I definitely fall in line with that.

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:41.910
When you now hear the ideas of socialism

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:44.810
being thrown around as if
this is the right set of ideas

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:47.060
or when you listen to say
Bernie or Elizabeth Warren,

00:22:47.060 --> 00:22:52.060
and the amount of government
involvement they want to use

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:57.090
to create their perfect utopian society,

00:22:57.090 --> 00:23:00.200
are you shocked that these
ideas gained traction?

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.630
- I am, I don't think shocked

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:06.383
would be the term I'd use, I'm distressed.

00:23:08.935 --> 00:23:10.900
I'm lucky enough to have been
friends with Milton Friedman,

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:14.240
who is maybe the greatest
economist for the last 200 years,

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:17.820
at least since Adam Smith,
and he was just a wise man

00:23:17.820 --> 00:23:20.330
in so many things.
- I was on the front page

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:22.090
of the New York Times in
a compilation with him

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:24.793
as some of the leaders of the far right.

00:23:24.793 --> 00:23:26.080
- Wow.
- That's how bananas

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:26.970
the media is.
- So cool.

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:30.200
- So I'm sympathetic to
your wife's situation.

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:34.210
- An amazing guy, and I remember
towards the end of his life

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.510
which was around the turn of the century,

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:39.250
he died in the early 2000s, he said to me,

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:40.537
and it was kind of in despair, he said,

00:23:40.537 --> 00:23:43.047
"Steve, what is the most enduring lesson

00:23:43.047 --> 00:23:44.747
"of the 20th century?"

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:47.640
I think we both agreed, I
think most people would agree,

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:50.200
the enduring lesson of the 20th century

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:54.670
is that communism,
socialism, Nazism, Maoism,

00:23:54.670 --> 00:23:57.340
Bernie Sanders-ism, whatever
you wanna call these things,

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:00.870
these government controls of
the economy, they're a failure.

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:04.315
And the free enterprise
system is a success.

00:24:04.315 --> 00:24:06.210
That's what everybody should have learned

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:07.910
over the last 100 years.

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:10.200
It's how the United States of America

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:14.460
became the most economically
powerful nation in the world.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.257
And then he said, "But
yet the lesson everyone

00:24:18.257 --> 00:24:20.428
"seems to have learned is
we need more socialism."

00:24:20.428 --> 00:24:22.600
(laughing)

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:25.213
So it is disturbing, we
have overwhelming evidence--

00:24:25.213 --> 00:24:26.290
- Do you think that's just lazy thinking?

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:27.530
What do you think that is?

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:29.260
Is it a branding of capitalism

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:30.120
that's wrong.
- Yes.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:32.580
- Have we grown fat on all of the excesses

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:33.662
because it's so freaking great.

00:24:33.662 --> 00:24:34.670
- Yes, I think both of those things.

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:35.800
You make two great points.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.920
Number one, capitalism, I don't really use

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:41.620
the term capitalism anymore
because it's seen as greed

00:24:42.794 --> 00:24:44.110
and the rich getting richer,

00:24:44.110 --> 00:24:45.870
so I think we've lost that term.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:49.030
I talk about free enterprise system

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:51.520
and let people start businesses,

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:52.900
let people do what they wanna do,

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.303
let the economy work itself out.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.330
The other point you make is I do think

00:24:58.330 --> 00:24:59.920
we've become fat and happy,

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:03.140
and especially the millennials
who, you've seen these polls,

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.940
52% of college graduates
now think that socialism

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:08.000
is superior to free market capitalism.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:11.003
I mean, how stupid is
that, to believe that?

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:13.580
Now part of that is the schools.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:17.100
We've done a horrible
job of allowing the left

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.990
to take over our government school system,

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:20.530
and now we're paying
a high price for that.

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:22.640
We're turning out kids who don't know

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:24.053
anything about anything.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:27.593
It's a big task we have at hand.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:30.740
One other thing, I don't even think

00:25:30.740 --> 00:25:32.510
we should call them socialists.

00:25:32.510 --> 00:25:33.590
People like Elizabeth Warren

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.333
and Bernie Sanders are authoritarians.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:39.870
You were saying that you
have Libertarian tendencies

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:40.703
and I do, too.

00:25:40.703 --> 00:25:42.730
I don't want the government
to tell me what to do.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:43.563
- That's it.

00:25:43.563 --> 00:25:45.200
- They do want the government,

00:25:45.200 --> 00:25:46.100
they want the government to tell you

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:47.210
what kind of straw you can use,

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:48.750
what kind of light bulb
you can use in your home,

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:51.060
where you have to set your thermostat at,

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:52.459
what kind of car you can drive--

00:25:52.459 --> 00:25:53.419
What kind of school you can go to.

00:25:53.419 --> 00:25:54.750
- What kind of school, I
mean, they want control

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.833
of everything, and when
I talk to young people,

00:25:56.833 --> 00:25:58.970
this is my plea, I'm
so glad to do this show

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:01.060
'cause I know you've got a great audience,

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.990
for young people watching this show,

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:06.750
freedom and liberty are
precious commodities.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.040
Most people in the world don't have

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.890
the freedoms and liberties
that you and I have

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:11.460
or the people watching the show.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.400
Guard those jealously.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.340
Millions of Americans
have given up their lives

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:18.800
to safeguard those freedoms and liberties.

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:21.040
And now we've got a whole
generation of kids who's willing

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:24.000
to just throw those
liberties and freedoms away.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:25.200
It distresses me.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.560
- Well, one of the
things that I always find

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.250
so confusing about it is that
the arguments run so thin.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:30.480
So just in the last couple of weeks,

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.580
AOC was giving a speech
about how she didn't want

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:34.985
Mark Zuckerberg to be in
charge of her healthcare.

00:26:34.985 --> 00:26:35.967
- (laughing) Exactly.

00:26:35.967 --> 00:26:37.270
- Now I get it, I don't
really want Mark Zuckerberg

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:38.103
to be in charge of my healthcare.

00:26:38.103 --> 00:26:39.670
I don't know exactly
why she was saying that,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.540
but it's like, well, what she wants

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:43.440
is Bernie to be in charge
of your healthcare.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:45.500
And I don't know, who's
worse outta those two?

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:47.330
- You know who should be in
charge of your healthcare?

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:49.270
- You!
- You, exactly.

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:50.103
We should all be.

00:26:50.103 --> 00:26:51.790
This is the danger, I
mean, think about this.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:53.340
The two industries, I talk about this

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:54.590
in every speech I give,

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:56.100
what two industries in America

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.170
are most dominated by government?

00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:00.160
- Healthcare and--
- And education.

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:01.240
- And education, okay.
- Yeah.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:02.230
These are the two industries

00:27:02.230 --> 00:27:04.570
we've basically turned
over to the government.

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:05.480
Look, our healthcare system

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:07.043
is 50% private and 50% government.

00:27:07.043 --> 00:27:09.310
- And now the progressives
don't want school choice either,

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:10.570
which is completely insane.
- Of course.

00:27:10.570 --> 00:27:12.600
So think about this though,
so you got the two industries

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:13.660
that are most run by government,

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:15.740
guess which two industries
have had the biggest increase

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.180
in cost over the last 50 years?

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.025
Education and healthcare.
- I think I'm with you here.

00:27:19.025 --> 00:27:20.310
- You don't have to be a genius here.

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:21.957
So wait a minute, and then
I ask my liberal friends,

00:27:21.957 --> 00:27:23.927
"Why do we have the government
play such a big role

00:27:23.927 --> 00:27:24.910
"in education and healthcare?"

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:25.743
You know what they say?

00:27:25.743 --> 00:27:27.090
"To make it more affordable."

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:29.310
Gee, they're doing a wonderful
job with it, aren't they?

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.480
So why not let every kid,

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:33.590
you and I have to start a movement,

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:36.380
every child in America,
whether they're black, brown,

00:27:36.380 --> 00:27:39.500
whatever color, whatever income
range, wherever they live,

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:43.260
it is a disgrace that we're
sending millions of kids

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:44.610
to failing schools.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:47.040
Why not allow every single
American to have the right

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:49.550
that Barack Obama had when
he moved to Washington, D.C.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.160
and moved into public housing,
right, in the White House?

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:52.993
What did he do?

00:27:52.993 --> 00:27:54.900
He didn't send his kids
to the public schools.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:57.290
He sent them to elite private schools.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:28:00.840
Why can't we let every single
American have that same right

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:03.640
and have the schools compete for kids?

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:06.140
Competition is a wonderful thing.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:07.030
- Literally just this morning,

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.560
we're gonna hold this
video for a couple of days,

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.620
but I just saw Elizabeth
Warren tweet this morning

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:13.380
about how she basically wants
to take out charter schools.

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:15.130
- She did?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:16.934
- Oh my god, that is so distressing.

00:28:16.934 --> 00:28:17.767
- I just retweeted it this morning.

00:28:17.767 --> 00:28:20.530
- It's like they wanna force people...

00:28:20.530 --> 00:28:21.700
This gets to the whole idea

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:23.000
you and I were just talking about.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:26.360
Who can make a better
decision about what schools

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:27.780
their kids should go to,

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:30.080
Elizabeth Warren or the
parents of those kids?

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.330
Who do you think cares
more about those kids?

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:34.640
- To give the devil his due here,

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:38.190
what do you think the root of
what these people think is?

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.440
So okay, we can sit here and agree

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:42.090
that their ideas are bad, let's say,

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:42.990
but they obviously don't believe that.

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:44.220
- I think they have a trust in government.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:46.870
- They just have a fundamental
trust in something else.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:51.350
- Well, look, I think there's
a sinister answer to that

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:55.220
and then I do think some just
believe government is good,

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:57.260
and all we need is just
to spend more money

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:58.093
on the public schools.

00:28:58.093 --> 00:28:59.720
That's all we've been
doing for the last 50 years

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:01.410
and they're getting worse, not better.

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:02.620
So my attitude is just from,

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:04.700
I don't care what your
political philosophy is,

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:06.270
if you keep doing something over and over,

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:08.460
and it doesn't work, you
stop doing it, right?

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.320
But the other thing is,
let's be honest about this.

00:29:11.320 --> 00:29:13.100
Who runs the government schools?

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:14.380
The teachers' unions do.

00:29:14.380 --> 00:29:15.730
What is the most power...

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:19.040
35% of the delegates in
the Democratic Convention

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:20.600
are teachers' unions' members.

00:29:20.600 --> 00:29:22.883
They run the Democratic party.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:26.870
Unfortunately, too many Democrats

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:29.653
put the interest of the teachers'
unions ahead of the kids

00:29:29.653 --> 00:29:31.989
that we have the schools that
are supposed to be served.

00:29:31.989 --> 00:29:33.895
- All right, we literally only have five--

00:29:33.895 --> 00:29:35.235
- No, no, I was just getting going here!

00:29:35.235 --> 00:29:36.130
- I know, I know, we only
have five minutes left.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:36.963
- I'm teasing.

00:29:36.963 --> 00:29:38.161
- 'Cause you gotta get to Fox, it's okay.

00:29:38.161 --> 00:29:39.740
- Next time I'm in LA, can
I come back on the show?

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:40.815
I'd love to keep talking to you.

00:29:40.815 --> 00:29:41.648
- Yes, you can absolutely come back

00:29:41.648 --> 00:29:43.070
and we'll do it with no time limit.

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:45.540
But in five minutes, this is
gonna be almost impossible,

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:48.190
can you give me a sorta basic breakdown

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:49.670
of where we're at with China?

00:29:49.670 --> 00:29:51.110
I think this is pretty much

00:29:51.110 --> 00:29:52.900
the most confusing thing right now.

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:55.470
People have no idea what is
being ramped up right now.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:59.210
- So it's not just confusing,
this is the issue of our time.

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:00.540
I've called this the--
- Well, congratulations.

00:30:00.540 --> 00:30:01.550
I've given you five minutes.

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:03.480
- Yeah, I know, it's the
epic battle of our time.

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:07.950
Just as from the 1950s
through the late '80s,

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:11.840
it was the Cold War with the Soviet Union,

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.460
now we have an epic battle,

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:16.100
I think this is the new
Cold War, I really do.

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:19.050
I think that this is World War
IV (chuckles) that we've got.

00:30:19.050 --> 00:30:20.370
I mean, China is--

00:30:20.370 --> 00:30:21.780
- Wait, why'd you skipped three there?

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:22.613
- Pardon me?

00:30:22.613 --> 00:30:23.446
- Why'd you skip World War III?

00:30:23.446 --> 00:30:24.710
- Well, I think you could say the Cold War

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.650
was sorta World War III.
- Oh, okay.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:29.260
- But in any case, I mean,
I'm exaggerating a little bit,

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:31.040
but the point I'm making is,

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:34.570
China is becoming increasingly
a menace on the world scene.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:37.400
The march towards freedom,
look, Mao was the single

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:40.220
greatest villain maybe who ever
walked on the planet earth.

00:30:40.220 --> 00:30:43.230
I mean, he killed tens
and tens of millions

00:30:43.230 --> 00:30:44.333
of his own people.

00:30:45.251 --> 00:30:47.390
And then finally they
moved away from communism,

00:30:47.390 --> 00:30:48.500
which we were talking about before,

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.840
and they started to embrace privatization

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:52.190
and free enterprise, not fully,

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:53.740
but they moved in that direction.

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:54.573
And they had a boom!

00:30:54.573 --> 00:30:56.790
I mean, one of the great
booms ever in history

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:58.800
what happened in China
over the last 30 years.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:00.680
The problem has been, they've moved more

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:03.000
in a Stalinistic direction now.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:04.440
So they're building up their military,

00:31:04.440 --> 00:31:05.660
they're cheating on trade deals,

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:08.550
they've never opened
up their markets to us.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:11.090
The way I describe it to Donald Trump,

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:14.440
not that he needed any urging
on this, 'cause he got this,

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:16.960
we are in an abusive trade
relationship with China.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:18.940
We've opened up our markets to them,

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:20.750
they have not opened
up their markets to us.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.730
They're stealing our
intellectual property.

00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:25.830
I applaud Trump for finally
calling them out on this.

00:31:25.830 --> 00:31:27.140
It's about time.

00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:30.110
Where this is right now,
the ball's in their court.

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:33.250
We have a mini deal right
now, but look, China could...

00:31:33.250 --> 00:31:35.630
You can't trust these people
as far as you could spit.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:38.010
I mean, the problem is they
also don't keep their word.

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:41.440
So it's a tricky situation,
but this is a battle

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:43.620
that's not gonna be resolved
in the next three months

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.250
or six months or a year or two.

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:47.220
It's probably gonna go
on for a decade or two,

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:51.310
and the big issue is, will
the United States or China

00:31:51.310 --> 00:31:53.220
be the world economic superpower

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:54.620
over the next 25 or 30 years?

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:56.440
You know who I'm gonna put my money on?

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:57.314
Us.
- I think you're putting it on

00:31:57.314 --> 00:31:58.180
the U.S.
- I'm putting it on us.

00:31:58.180 --> 00:31:59.414
You know why?

00:31:59.414 --> 00:32:00.990
'Cause we still do have
a free enterprise system.

00:32:00.990 --> 00:32:02.733
They are command and control.

00:32:04.085 --> 00:32:06.473
They have the Elizabeth
Warren type of economy.

00:32:06.473 --> 00:32:07.306
It doesn't work.

00:32:07.306 --> 00:32:08.190
- So you think ultimately that sorta thing

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:11.080
might work at some level
for some period of time,

00:32:11.080 --> 00:32:11.913
but ultimately it creates that.

00:32:11.913 --> 00:32:13.275
- It fails.

00:32:13.275 --> 00:32:14.560
Show me any time, I would
say to my liberal friends,

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:15.920
show me any time, any place,

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:18.410
anywhere where that model has worked?

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:20.240
It didn't work for the Soviet Union,

00:32:20.240 --> 00:32:21.450
it hasn't worked for North Korea,

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:24.050
it didn't work for Hugo
Chavez in Venezuela,

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:25.450
and it didn't work for Castro.

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:27.320
I mean, it's never worked anywhere,

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:29.530
because the genius of the
free enterprise system

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:32.030
is millions of actors who make decisions.

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:34.450
The idea that you can
have a few politicians

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:37.110
in a central politburo
that can make the decisions

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:41.570
about an economy with millions
and millions of decisions,

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:44.270
it's ludicrous, it will break down.

00:32:44.270 --> 00:32:46.570
I'm putting my money on Silicon Valley

00:32:46.570 --> 00:32:47.830
and the American--
- They've got their own

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:50.700
set of problems though because
of how lefty they've gone.

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:52.070
But that's a whole other topic

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:53.150
that we'll have to discuss next time.

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:54.410
I think you proved to
me you're an economist.

00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:56.790
- Wait, one other big
advantage over China,

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:58.610
I always say this in my speeches,

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:00.542
our Chinese are smarter
than their Chinese.

00:33:00.542 --> 00:33:03.021
(laughing)

00:33:03.021 --> 00:33:04.280
- I think you proved to me
you're an economist here

00:33:04.280 --> 00:33:07.040
because we probably got
the most amount of ideas

00:33:07.040 --> 00:33:08.043
packed into the shortest
show I've ever done.

00:33:08.043 --> 00:33:09.510
- It's called efficiency, my friend.

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:11.580
- That is efficiency, there you go.

